Two windows are two toplevels on gtk4 and fyne: two nodes at the root of the
package body, each attached to the application, and each opened and closed by
its `#id`.

A window the window manager closes is reported hidden, which writes its
`visible`, and then runs its `@closed`. `log` keeps its own `visible`, so a
close hides it and counts; `main` binds nothing either and hides the same way,
and the program ends when a close leaves no window on screen.

A reactive `if` at the root of a window's content is a render slot parented to
the window's Toplevel like one parented to any other container.

On html `main` is the document and `log` a `<dialog>` in it: the same calls
open and close it, its header's close button is the window manager's close,
and `main.close()` writes the cell `main` keeps for its `visible` while the
document stays where it is. The slot renders into an anchor inside the dialog.

deny * `gtk4rt\.Window\{|snglWindow|\.Mount\(\)|\.Unmount\(\)` -- a window is a Toplevel node, not a record kept beside the tree
deny * `__root1` -- a window's content has no box of its own outside the Toplevel

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        gtk4
        fyne
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

var closes = 0

ui.window #main(title="Main") {
    ui.text(value="closed the log {closes} times")
    ui.button(text="Log", @click {
        log.open()
    })
}

ui.window #log(title="Log", @closed {
    closes += 1
    log.close()
}) {
    ui.text(value="{closes}")
    // A reactive `if` at the root of the second window re-renders into that
    // window's own box, `__root1`, not the first window's.
    if closes > 2 {
        ui.text(value="closed often")
    }
    ui.button(text="Hide main", @click {
        main.close()
    })
}
